%% The Topic 7 key diagrams, redrawn simply — RN volume only (included by RN.gsm).

## The key diagrams

Four diagrams carry most of Topic 7. Learn to draw each one from memory in **under two minutes**. A diagram placed early in an answer shows the examiner the whole argument at a glance and earns credit in its own right.

::: fig pyramid | The ecological model (Heise, 1998), applied to Pakistan.
Society: patriarchy, honour codes, impunity
Community: jirgas, poverty, acceptance of violence
Relationship: husband controls money; joint-family pressure
Individual: saw violence as a child; low education
:::

**Draw it in:** root causes and how they intersect (2025). It also gives any strategies answer its structure.

::: fig cycle | The Duluth Power and Control Wheel.
@centre Power and control
Intimidation
Emotional abuse
Isolation
Minimise, deny, blame
Using children
Male privilege
Economic abuse
Coercion and threats
:::

**Draw it in:** power and control (2020). Say it: the **hub** is power and control, the **spokes** are tactics, and the **rim** — physical and sexual violence — holds the wheel together.

::: fig timeline | Pakistan's laws against violence — the anchors.
@layout vertical
1979: Hudood Ordinances
2004: honour killing defined
2006: Protection of Women Act
2010: workplace harassment law
2011: acid law; anti-women practices law
2013: Sindh domestic-violence law
2016: Punjab Protection Act; honour law; PECA
2021: Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act
2025: Islamabad domestic-violence and child-marriage laws
:::

**Draw it in:** the honour-killing note (2017), government initiatives (2018) and the Punjab Act 2016 (2024).

::: fig cycle | The five Ps — strategies to end GBV.
@centre Ending GBV
Prevention: norms, schools, men and boys
Protection: helplines, shelters, orders
Prosecution: FIRs, convictions
Provision: health, legal aid, livelihoods
Partnership: state, NGOs, ulema, media
:::

**Draw it in:** workable strategies (2017), practical strategies (2023) and the strategies half of 2016.
